From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] The ROM read on demand on Windows 95

ADR 0003 decided the ROM is read on demand rather than held: twelve megabytes
of a machine that may have thirty-two. When the validated ROM file is already
big-endian, load_stored_rom hands its path to set_rom_file; PI DMA and PIO
then read through a one-megabyte cache of 16 KiB blocks, and the image held
by select_rom is freed. get_rom() still works, by reading the image in full
and saying so in the log. load_mods asked for it on every start, for no mod
at all; on this target it now asks only when a mod is active.

Measured on the test machine in a race: 33.6 MB of free physical memory
instead of 23.0 MB, the frame period unchanged (35.3 ms against 36.3 ms in
the run before, inside the drift between sessions).

The cache's size, measured in the same race: 64 KiB took 2438 block misses
in 6144 reads, 256 KiB 746, 1 MiB 408 -- about the distinct blocks the race
touches -- and the frame period stayed within 0.2 ms across the three.
1 MiB is kept: a sixth of the misses, for a tenth of what was saved.
DKR_ROM_CACHE_BLOCKS=<1..64> uses fewer blocks, for measuring; the log
counts the reads and misses every 1024 reads.
